  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1226 PM MDT Wed Aug 19 2026\n\nWeak shortwave energy continues to propagate across the region this \nmorning. A sizable cumulus field has begun to sprout up on much of \nthe terrain already. Showers and thunderstorms are possible on most \nof the mountains this afternoon. Storm motions will slide northwest \nto southeast. Dry valleys will likely inherit a few gusty outflows \nfrom decaying storms this afternoon, but little in the way of \nrainfall. Flash flooding is unlikely today. However, stronger \nshowers on recent burn scars always warrant a watchful eye towards \nquickly changing conditions. Shower activity will wane this evening.\n\nHigh pressure will continue to evolve over the Southwest Thursday, \nwith little change from today's mountain shower regime. Temperatures \nwill respond accordingly, ratcheting upward in excess of 10 degrees \nover climatology Thursday and Friday.\nShowers and clouds will play a role in potential for afternoon highs \nacross the mountains. However, warm desert valleys will remain \ncharacteristically hot until they can tap into some moisture and \ncloud cover. Mountain showers will become more concentrated into \nFriday, especially on the San Juans, where additional moisture could \nincrease the risk of flash flood concerns.\n\nHigh pressure begins to shift eastward Saturday, introducing \nadditional moisture and dynamic forcing at the hands of shortwave \nripples in the mean flow. This is expected to produce another round \nof elevated flash flood potential. Models pull the high eastward \nSunday. This is expected to shift moisture and forcing.\nHowever, additional showers should be expected Sunday afternoon and \nbeyond, as models grapple with the downstream evolution of the high \npressure over the Plains. Temperatures will continue to trend around \nnormal for late August and afternoon showers continue on the \nmountains.",
